<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reich Chamber of Culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Reichskulturkammer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Reich Chamber of Culture was set up in September 1933 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a> to control cultural life in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Writers, musicians, artists, actors, film-makers and journalists had to belong to one of its seven chambers in order to work, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and politically suspect people were excluded, driving many into exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reich-chamber-of-culture.jpg" alt="Reich Chamber of Culture" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UnknownUnknown · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-H29353,_Joseph_Goebbels_im_Arbeitszimmer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
